1
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:05,940
Great

2
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:09,080
So we had some feedback and office hours and other times

3
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,800
that it might be helpful to have a high-level recap

4
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,280
of the algorithms that we've talked about so far

5
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:17,400
to try to understand where some of the pieces fit together

6
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,780
So I put together one slide that is a little bit more high level

7
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:23,480
but actually captures almost all of the algorithms

8
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:26,240
that we've seen so far

9
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:28,840
And in particular, we've talked about both online

10
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:34,280
and offline algorithms, where online refers to scenarios where

11
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:37,600
the policy will be used to collect data

12
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:40,520
And offline means that we're given a data set

13
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:43,560
And we cannot do any additional data collection

14
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:45,520
in our environment

15
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:48,720
And in the offline setting, we talked

16
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:51,200
about two different classes of reinforcement

17
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,320
learning methods that use on-policy data

18
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:55,400
collection in order to improve

19
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:57,660
We referred to the first type as on-policy RL

20
00:00:57,660 --> 00:01:00,070
and the second type as off-policy RL

21
00:01:00,070 --> 00:01:03,830
where on-policy RL, you can collect data but only

22
00:01:03,830 --> 00:01:06,010
from the current policy

23
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:07,930
You can only use data from the current policy

24
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:12,220
And this means that you are not going to be reusing past data

25
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:15,430
You can't use offline data and so forth

26
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:18,750
And this include algorithms REINFORCE or the vanilla policy

27
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:20,670
gradient method

28
00:01:20,670 --> 00:01:22,890
And then within the realm of off-policy RL

29
00:01:22,890 --> 00:01:25,730
this was where we could start to reuse data from other policies

30
00:01:25,730 --> 00:01:29,430
including data from policies before we took a gradient step

31
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:32,710
on our policy parameters

32
00:01:32,710 --> 00:01:35,150
And this included algorithms like PPO

33
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:37,570
and importance sampling

34
00:01:37,570 --> 00:01:39,030
And then we talked about algorithms

35
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:42,830
that are more off-policy that can reuse data from policies

36
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:46,710
that are even more different and where

37
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:48,710
we use a replay buffer to store the data

38
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:50,170
from those past policies

39
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:56,350
And that included algorithms like DQN and SAC

40
00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:58,610
And then I colored these different algorithms

41
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:02,150
different colors because the blue algorithms only

42
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:03,790
involve training a policy

43
00:02:03,790 --> 00:02:07,070
So we refer to them as policy gradient methods

44
00:02:07,070 --> 00:02:10,270
PPO and SAC learn both a policy and a critic

45
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:12,950
And so we refer to them as actor critic methods

46
00:02:12,950 --> 00:02:16,350
And there's also algorithms that only learn a critic

47
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:19,030
and don't explicitly learn a separate policy

48
00:02:19,030 --> 00:02:22,470
And those are often referred to as Q-learning algorithms

49
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:24,060
such as DQN

50
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:26,430
I should also mention that PPO is often actually referred

51
00:02:26,430 --> 00:02:29,550
to as a policy gradient method, although you do actually

52
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:31,450
learn a value function

53
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:33,150
And so we typically were referring to it

54
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:37,750
as actor critic in this class to avoid confusion

55
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:40,730
And then separate from these classes of online methods

56
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:45,190
we also have offline RL methods that we started talking about

57
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:48,510
And these built upon a lot of the ideas we saw in online RL

58
00:02:48,510 --> 00:02:50,510
But I have to cope with the fact that you're not

59
00:02:50,510 --> 00:02:52,730
collecting any data at all

60
00:02:52,730 --> 00:02:54,730
And we looked at two different approaches there

61
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:58,940
One is to only considering actions in the data

62
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:05,660
And this included algorithms like AWR, AWAC, and IQL and also

63
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:08,380
an algorithm like SQL that implicitly is constraining you

64
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:13,160
to actions in the data but not so explicitly

65
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:16,740
And this is through conservatism

66
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:21,980
Now, for these RL algorithms, generally, as you move right

67
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:24,180
you need more online data

68
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:27,020
And so that means that typically they're

69
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:29,780
going to be more expensive because online data is often

70
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:33,460
more expensive than offline data because it's harder

71
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:39,940
to repeatedly collect a large amount of online data

72
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:41,880
And then lastly, before we talked about RL

73
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:44,180
we also talked about imitation learning algorithms

74
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:48,820
And this can also fall within the bucket of offline

75
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:50,700
and online, although there's a bit

76
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:52,140
different set of assumptions

77
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:54,180
The first imitation learning algorithm we covered

78
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:57,410
was kind of vanilla behavior cloning, where

79
00:03:57,410 --> 00:03:59,290
we're taking a data set and trying to imitate

80
00:03:59,290 --> 00:04:01,410
the actions in that data set

81
00:04:01,410 --> 00:04:03,730
And then we also talked about an online algorithm

82
00:04:03,730 --> 00:04:06,970
that is online both in terms of the policy

83
00:04:06,970 --> 00:04:09,850
and in terms of querying an expert

84
00:04:09,850 --> 00:04:12,130
like asking an expert what is the right action

85
00:04:12,130 --> 00:04:15,290
on this new data that I'm collecting

86
00:04:15,290 --> 00:04:16,950
And I put these in--

87
00:04:16,950 --> 00:04:18,870
they're in a little bit of a separate category

88
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:21,070
because they have different assumptions

89
00:04:21,070 --> 00:04:23,650
You don't need a reward function

90
00:04:23,650 --> 00:04:26,090
But you do need pretty good data

91
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:28,650
And you're not going to be able to outperform

92
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:33,190
the average behavior in your data set

93
00:04:33,190 --> 00:04:33,690
Cool

94
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:37,210
So that's an overview including a lot of these different terms

95
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:39,010
that we see in reinforcement learning

96
00:04:39,010 --> 00:04:43,250
like offline, online, off-policy, on-policy

97
00:04:43,250 --> 00:04:44,850
and so forth, and imitation learning

98
00:04:44,850 --> 00:04:47,730
versus reinforcement learning

99
00:04:47,730 --> 00:04:48,730
Cool

100
00:04:48,730 --> 00:04:50,450
So what we're going to talk about today

101
00:04:50,450 --> 00:04:52,700
is we're going to talk about model-based reinforcement

102
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:53,450
learning

103
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:56,090
And model-based reinforcement learning

104
00:04:56,090 --> 00:04:59,170
can actually be applied to all of the different classes

105
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:02,790
of reinforcement learning methods on the previous slide

106
00:05:02,790 --> 00:05:04,410
Someone has a special place in my heart

107
00:05:04,410 --> 00:05:07,450
because I used to do actually quite a bit of work

108
00:05:07,450 --> 00:05:08,360
on model-based RL

109
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:10,530
And I think that there's a lot of really cool things

110
00:05:10,530 --> 00:05:13,370
that you can do with model-based reinforcement learning

111
00:05:13,370 --> 00:05:17,230
We'll talk about the key idea behind it, how to learn models

112
00:05:17,230 --> 00:05:20,370
how to use those models, including with planning and data

113
00:05:20,370 --> 00:05:21,490
generation

114
00:05:21,490 --> 00:05:24,490
And then we'll also look at one case study in dexterous robot

115
00:05:24,490 --> 00:05:25,810
manipulation

116
00:05:25,810 --> 00:05:28,480
And as a teaser for that, the methods

117
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:29,850
that we'll be talking about today

118
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:33,310
is we'll allow a robot to do something like this

119
00:05:33,310 --> 00:05:34,190
which is pretty cool

120
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:35,730
This hand has more than 20 degrees

121
00:05:35,730 --> 00:05:37,730
of freedom, more than 20 different joints that's

122
00:05:37,730 --> 00:05:40,130
controlling

123
00:05:40,130 --> 00:05:42,550
And then the key outcomes is trying

124
00:05:42,550 --> 00:05:44,130
to understand how we can learn and use

125
00:05:44,130 --> 00:05:46,410
dynamics models and the different challenges

126
00:05:46,410 --> 00:05:51,530
and trade-offs with this whole class of methods

127
00:05:51,530 --> 00:05:52,720
Cool

128
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:54,720
So the key idea behind model-based reinforcement

129
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,960
learning is essentially we're going

130
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:59,800
to try to learn a simulator

131
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:04,560
Can we learn how to predict the future for actions

132
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:07,200
that we might be taking

133
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:12,720
And for robotics and driving and other physical systems

134
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:15,640
this might be modeling the physics

135
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:18,640
of some particular system if you can directly

136
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:24,320
observe the underlying physical entities in that system

137
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:27,040
And alternatively, if you can't directly

138
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:30,000
observe the different entities in that physical system

139
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
and model them, then this might look something

140
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,920
more like video prediction conditioned on actions

141
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:37,880
that the agent wants to take

142
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:40,560
So for example, I might want to predict--

143
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:44,500
if I say something to someone else, how will they react

144
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:47,780
Or if I, for example, move my legs in a certain way

145
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:48,780
will I fall down

146
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:49,580
Will I go that way

147
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:51,000
Will I go that way

148
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,680
And so forth

149
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:57,040
In terms of what this video production might actually

150
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:01,320
look like, we've seen pretty cool videos coming out

151
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:04,720
of large models like Veo 2 and Sora

152
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:10,640
And these are examples of videos that are

153
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:12,360
generated by a neural network

154
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:14,400
You could imagine that these kinds of models

155
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:19,740
could be very useful for training systems like cars

156
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:25,080
robots, quadrotors that are flying through different places

157
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:30,600
in order to learn about how their actions affect the world

158
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:32,780
Now, models could look something like models

159
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:33,700
of the physical world

160
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:35,660
They could also look at models of other things

161
00:07:35,660 --> 00:07:38,560
So for example, if you wanted to learn a policy

162
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:40,620
to make money on the stock market

163
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:42,880
then one of these simulators would

164
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,600
be trying to predict what will happen to the stock

165
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:51,430
market over time and if your actions have any effect on that

166
00:07:51,430 --> 00:07:54,550
And then if you're trying to train an agent to play a game

167
00:07:54,550 --> 00:07:57,410
then this sort of model will underscore

168
00:07:57,410 --> 00:07:59,470
the rules of that game

169
00:07:59,470 --> 00:08:00,970
And actually, in a lot of games, you

170
00:08:00,970 --> 00:08:03,090
might actually already know the rules of the game

171
00:08:03,090 --> 00:08:04,510
And you may not even need to learn

172
00:08:04,510 --> 00:08:05,990
a model in the first place

173
00:08:05,990 --> 00:08:08,310
For example, in chess, if you take a particular action

174
00:08:08,310 --> 00:08:09,420
you know the outcome

175
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:13,550
You know how the piece will move as a result of that action

176
00:08:13,550 --> 00:08:15,750
And then it comes down to--

177
00:08:15,750 --> 00:08:18,150
in two player games or three player games or multiplayer

178
00:08:18,150 --> 00:08:22,300
games, you might need to model other players

179
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:24,630
Modeling other players-- you can sometimes consider that

180
00:08:24,630 --> 00:08:25,650
as part of the dynamics

181
00:08:25,650 --> 00:08:28,590
But oftentimes, it's actually modeled separately

182
00:08:28,590 --> 00:08:31,310
So you don't even necessarily need

183
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:34,750
to consider it as part of this simulator of the world

184
00:08:34,750 --> 00:08:36,470
And for example, you might be able to use

185
00:08:36,470 --> 00:08:43,049
your own model of yourself as a model of another agent

186
00:08:43,049 --> 00:08:46,710
So if we can learn a simulator, how would we use it

187
00:08:46,710 --> 00:08:49,180
So the sketch of an algorithm would

188
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:52,380
be something like this, where we get some data

189
00:08:52,380 --> 00:08:55,660
Then we learn a simulator on that data that's

190
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:59,580
going to trying to predict the future

191
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:02,860
for the present and some action that I

192
00:09:02,860 --> 00:09:06,180
might take from the present

193
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:08,300
And then try to run your favorite method

194
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:13,300
inside the simulator

195
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:16,700
So for example, maybe you have a simple scenario

196
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:19,380
where you want to get from the green point to the red point

197
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:23,020
You might collect some data

198
00:09:23,020 --> 00:09:25,740
This is collected from some policy

199
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:29,060
Then you would train some neural network conditioned

200
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:31,640
on a state and an action

201
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:34,180
What is the next state

202
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:36,180
And one way that you could learn this is just

203
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:39,620
by minimizing the L2 loss between

204
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:42,540
your predicted next state and the next state

205
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:45,020
observed in the data

206
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:48,360
And then in terms of running your favorite RL method

207
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,820
you could use one of the RL methods that we've covered

208
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:52,880
Or you could use a planning method

209
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:59,020
And we'll talk about a couple planning methods today

210
00:09:59,020 --> 00:09:59,520
Cool

211
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:00,440
So now, I have a question for you

212
00:10:00,440 --> 00:10:02,340
So here's a sketch of an algorithm

213
00:10:02,340 --> 00:10:05,060
Does anyone have thoughts on what might go wrong

214
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:07,520
if you follow this sketch

215
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:08,020
Yeah

216
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:12,460
So the idea there was that your simulator might not always

217
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:13,120
be accurate

218
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:16,140
And so similar to what we saw in reward modeling and also

219
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:19,500
arguably what we saw when training Q-functions

220
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,640
you might exploit those inaccuracies

221
00:10:21,640 --> 00:10:23,360
Those inaccuracies might compound

222
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:26,780
And then what you've learned in your learned simulator

223
00:10:26,780 --> 00:10:29,780
might not transfer to the real scenario

224
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:33,580
that you're trying to learn a good policy in

225
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:36,440
So there's also this data coverage issue

226
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:38,540
So similar to what we've seen when

227
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:40,830
training Q-functions and other things

228
00:10:40,830 --> 00:10:45,300
if this pi 0, if this initial policy

229
00:10:45,300 --> 00:10:47,910
is different from the policy that you're ultimately learning

230
00:10:47,910 --> 00:10:49,580
then you're going to have this mismatch

231
00:10:49,580 --> 00:10:51,610
And you might not have coverage--

232
00:10:51,610 --> 00:10:54,810
your model might not be accurate for the states that

233
00:10:54,810 --> 00:10:58,210
are visited by the policy that you're learning

234
00:10:58,210 --> 00:11:04,930
So this is mostly basically what's on this slide exactly

235
00:11:04,930 --> 00:11:07,450
So if you have some environment--

236
00:11:07,450 --> 00:11:09,930
maybe you want to navigate a maze--

237
00:11:09,930 --> 00:11:13,880
then you might collect some data set from some policy

238
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:16,170
Maybe the policy does something like this yellow arrow

239
00:11:16,170 --> 00:11:18,850
This is your data set

240
00:11:18,850 --> 00:11:21,770
But this data set doesn't cover the whole maze

241
00:11:21,770 --> 00:11:24,150
And so then if you wanted to go to this part of the maze

242
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:25,970
for example, or this part of the maze

243
00:11:25,970 --> 00:11:29,730
then your model probably wouldn't be very accurate

244
00:11:29,730 --> 00:11:32,590
about the mazes in those parts of the environment

245
00:11:32,590 --> 00:11:35,290
And so then you wouldn't be able to learn a policy for reaching

246
00:11:35,290 --> 00:11:38,210
those parts of the maze

247
00:11:38,210 --> 00:11:40,890
And then for the second point, you might not always

248
00:11:40,890 --> 00:11:42,030
have an accurate simulator

249
00:11:42,030 --> 00:11:45,760
So here's an example of a video generated by Sora

250
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:47,320
with the prompt, a humanoid robot

251
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:50,100
standing near a table with a red, green, and blue cubes on it

252
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:52,600
performs a cube stacking task with red on the bottom

253
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:54,200
and blue on the top

254
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:57,400
And you can see that the video generation does

255
00:11:57,400 --> 00:11:59,300
some creative things with physics

256
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:02,920
where it kind of changes the colors of the block

257
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:06,560
and synthesizes blocks out of thin air

258
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:09,440
I would love for my robot to be able to do something like this

259
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:10,880
Seems very useful

260
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:14,720
But this is not modeling what robots can actually

261
00:12:14,720 --> 00:12:17,280
do in reality

262
00:12:17,280 --> 00:12:19,360
And to this point, when you're actually

263
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:21,840
trying to develop this kind of model

264
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:25,160
oftentimes you might need to make choices

265
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:26,660
that are dependent on the domain

266
00:12:26,660 --> 00:12:28,700
like if you're trying to train a video prediction

267
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:33,640
model versus training a model of low-level physics

268
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:36,420
with friction or training a model of the stock market

269
00:12:36,420 --> 00:12:37,880
You might have some choices to make

270
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:40,040
in terms of what are the inputs to that model

271
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:41,500
and how are you training it, what's

272
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,440
the architecture, and so forth

273
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:45,440
And some domains are going to be much easier

274
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:48,520
to simulate than others

275
00:12:48,520 --> 00:12:51,000
And then lastly, for this last part

276
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,520
even if you have a really good simulator

277
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:59,080
this doesn't mean that it'll be easy to get a good policy

278
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:03,100
or to get actions that are accurate as a result

279
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:07,360
You still have to do the RL process using the simulator

280
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:10,560
And you need to account for inaccuracies in the model

281
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:12,200
during this process

282
00:13:12,200 --> 00:13:15,620
Because usually, your model is not perfect

283
00:13:15,620 --> 00:13:19,120
You don't have a perfect simulator of the world

284
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:22,280
That's the key idea behind model-based RL

285
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:23,920
Now, let's talk a little briefly about

286
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:25,990
how to learn a dynamics model

287
00:13:25,990 --> 00:13:27,740
We don't have too much time to cover this

288
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:28,900
So we'll just talk about it briefly

289
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:30,720
And then we'll spend most of the time

290
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:35,120
on how to use a dynamics model

291
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:39,440
So in terms of learning a good simulator or a good dynamics

292
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:43,030
model, there are some scenarios where you might know it already

293
00:13:43,030 --> 00:13:46,670
And you could just use it

294
00:13:46,670 --> 00:13:50,150
And then you're in great shape

295
00:13:50,150 --> 00:13:52,110
There's also scenarios where you might actually

296
00:13:52,110 --> 00:13:55,270
know most of the model, like if you

297
00:13:55,270 --> 00:14:00,590
are in a simplified robotic control setting

298
00:14:00,590 --> 00:14:02,930
where you're trying to push an object on a table

299
00:14:02,930 --> 00:14:04,010
Maybe you know most of it

300
00:14:04,010 --> 00:14:07,190
But you don't know the friction coefficient of the table

301
00:14:07,190 --> 00:14:12,990
Or in driving, maybe you have a pretty good model

302
00:14:12,990 --> 00:14:16,250
of how the car is going to accelerate

303
00:14:16,250 --> 00:14:22,830
how it will move forward with a given steering command

304
00:14:22,830 --> 00:14:24,170
and a gearing a given thing

305
00:14:24,170 --> 00:14:27,630
And you just need to model some additional things

306
00:14:27,630 --> 00:14:29,470
In those cases, you might actually

307
00:14:29,470 --> 00:14:32,950
be able to leverage knowledge that you know about the world

308
00:14:32,950 --> 00:14:36,270
and then fit those unknown parameters using the data

309
00:14:36,270 --> 00:14:38,950
This is actually, I think, one of the cooler ways

310
00:14:38,950 --> 00:14:41,870
to try to incorporate domain-specific knowledge

311
00:14:41,870 --> 00:14:45,790
into reinforcement learning algorithms

312
00:14:45,790 --> 00:14:48,910
And then in almost all scenarios in practice

313
00:14:48,910 --> 00:14:52,430
you don't really know the model

314
00:14:52,430 --> 00:14:55,710
And this might be because you can't-- for example

315
00:14:55,710 --> 00:14:58,610
if you're trying to manipulate some objects

316
00:14:58,610 --> 00:15:00,850
maybe you kind of know roughly the laws of physics

317
00:15:00,850 --> 00:15:02,910
But you don't know the vast majority

318
00:15:02,910 --> 00:15:05,420
of how an object is going to behave because it has

319
00:15:05,420 --> 00:15:06,630
lots of different properties

320
00:15:06,630 --> 00:15:08,660
And there's many different objects

321
00:15:08,660 --> 00:15:11,330
Or you don't know how people are going to interact in the world

322
00:15:11,330 --> 00:15:13,550
Or other drivers are going to behave in the world

323
00:15:13,550 --> 00:15:15,230
and so forth

324
00:15:15,230 --> 00:15:18,310
And in these scenarios, the typical practice

325
00:15:18,310 --> 00:15:21,070
is to learn the model from scratch

326
00:15:21,070 --> 00:15:28,470
with a neural network or some other function approximator

327
00:15:28,470 --> 00:15:32,970
Now, there are a couple options here as well

328
00:15:32,970 --> 00:15:38,630
So if you are using image observations, for example

329
00:15:38,630 --> 00:15:42,250
and you're trying to predict--

330
00:15:42,250 --> 00:15:47,960
if your state space, for example, is an image of--

331
00:15:47,960 --> 00:15:48,500
I don't know

332
00:15:48,500 --> 00:15:51,540
Maybe this is like you have a

333
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:55,540
Car and there's other cars here

334
00:15:55,540 --> 00:16:00,820
And you're trying to predict in video where those cars will

335
00:16:00,820 --> 00:16:02,920
be moving in the future

336
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:06,290
And maybe there's, I don't know, some trash on the ground

337
00:16:06,290 --> 00:16:13,220
or something like that

338
00:16:13,220 --> 00:16:17,140
I'll refer to this image as st. One option

339
00:16:17,140 --> 00:16:23,340
is to generate entire future video

340
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:26,320
like the videos that we saw on the previous slide

341
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:32,740
This would be conditioned on actions

342
00:16:32,740 --> 00:16:36,220
But this might be fairly expensive to do

343
00:16:36,220 --> 00:16:38,690
Because if you have to basically be

344
00:16:38,690 --> 00:16:41,770
predicting this entire video over time

345
00:16:41,770 --> 00:16:42,990
that's a pretty large thing

346
00:16:42,990 --> 00:16:45,130
And you have to model all the different details

347
00:16:45,130 --> 00:16:50,010
of how all of the pixels are going to change in the future

348
00:16:50,010 --> 00:16:52,970
And so one alternative option that is sometimes

349
00:16:52,970 --> 00:16:56,610
used to learn dynamics models is instead

350
00:16:56,610 --> 00:16:59,370
of modeling in the image space, you

351
00:16:59,370 --> 00:17:04,369
could instead learn some lower dimensional representation

352
00:17:04,369 --> 00:17:07,170
and then model in that representation space

353
00:17:07,170 --> 00:17:11,750
So you, for example, for a given state

354
00:17:11,750 --> 00:17:14,170
you could learn some sort of encoder

355
00:17:14,170 --> 00:17:18,250
that maps this to a lower dimensional representation

356
00:17:18,250 --> 00:17:18,750
space

357
00:17:18,750 --> 00:17:22,010
Maybe we'll refer to this as zt

358
00:17:22,010 --> 00:17:24,970
And then instead of learning a model in the original image

359
00:17:24,970 --> 00:17:30,130
space, we could then try to learn a model that predicts

360
00:17:30,130 --> 00:17:38,410
future zt's, like zt plus 1

361
00:17:38,410 --> 00:17:40,690
This can be a good alternative in scenarios

362
00:17:40,690 --> 00:17:43,290
where it seems like it'll be very expensive to learn

363
00:17:43,290 --> 00:17:44,300
a full model

364
00:17:44,300 --> 00:17:46,050
It also has its own challenges because you

365
00:17:46,050 --> 00:17:48,330
need to figure out how to get this representation

366
00:17:48,330 --> 00:17:49,770
in the first place

367
00:17:49,770 --> 00:17:52,570
And if you have a good way to get good representations

368
00:17:52,570 --> 00:17:55,250
it can be a good choice

369
00:17:55,250 --> 00:17:57,650
So the question was we are going to be losing information

370
00:17:57,650 --> 00:17:58,990
by going to a lower dimension

371
00:17:58,990 --> 00:18:03,130
And so, is the main reason to do this in terms of cost

372
00:18:03,130 --> 00:18:03,950
Yeah, and exactly

373
00:18:03,950 --> 00:18:05,580
So the main reason to do this is to try

374
00:18:05,580 --> 00:18:08,130
to reduce some of the computational costs associated

375
00:18:08,130 --> 00:18:12,250
with this because you might be losing information

376
00:18:12,250 --> 00:18:13,770
And the key idea is that there is

377
00:18:13,770 --> 00:18:17,650
some parts of this observation that doesn't matter, perhaps

378
00:18:17,650 --> 00:18:20,730
like the exact texture of the road

379
00:18:20,730 --> 00:18:24,490
whether it's really dark black or maybe a little bit worn down

380
00:18:24,490 --> 00:18:25,990
In masonries, that might not matter

381
00:18:25,990 --> 00:18:27,870
And so if you can learn a representation that

382
00:18:27,870 --> 00:18:30,570
discards that information that's irrelevant

383
00:18:30,570 --> 00:18:33,370
then you don't have to spend so much compute modeling

384
00:18:33,370 --> 00:18:35,980
all those details that don't matter

385
00:18:35,980 --> 00:18:37,560
The other thing that I'll mention here

386
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:41,380
is that oftentimes when you're trying to learn these models

387
00:18:41,380 --> 00:18:43,760
typically with supervised learning or generative modeling

388
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:47,880
techniques, you often will also need to learn a reward model as

389
00:18:47,880 --> 00:18:48,780
well

390
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:50,360
And the reason for this is that if you

391
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:54,120
want to be using your model to generate data

392
00:18:54,120 --> 00:18:56,760
or to see into the future, you need

393
00:18:56,760 --> 00:18:59,020
to see into the future not just the states

394
00:18:59,020 --> 00:19:04,760
but also how those predicted states will lead to rewards

395
00:19:04,760 --> 00:19:06,840
And so we'll talk about what that looks

396
00:19:06,840 --> 00:19:10,500
like on the next couple slides

397
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:12,000
The question is, does model learning

398
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,760
have to happen end to end

399
00:19:14,760 --> 00:19:19,240
The way that it works is we are optimizing for our ability

400
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:22,640
to generate future states

401
00:19:22,640 --> 00:19:25,960
And there are actually pros and cons

402
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:28,600
that we'll talk about with regard to that objective

403
00:19:28,600 --> 00:19:32,660
But one way to think about it is like

404
00:19:32,660 --> 00:19:35,710
how well can I model the distribution

405
00:19:35,710 --> 00:19:40,920
of P st plus 1 given st, at

406
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:42,630
Another way to think about it, especially

407
00:19:42,630 --> 00:19:44,750
if you have more deterministic dynamics

408
00:19:44,750 --> 00:19:52,030
is, how well can I minimize the error

409
00:19:52,030 --> 00:20:02,830
between my predicted st plus 1 and the actual st plus 1

410
00:20:02,830 --> 00:20:09,730
So how do we go about using a dynamics model

411
00:20:09,730 --> 00:20:11,510
The first approach that we'll talk about

412
00:20:11,510 --> 00:20:13,590
is we'll actually start by talking about planning

413
00:20:13,590 --> 00:20:16,010
And so this is actually going to be a little bit different

414
00:20:16,010 --> 00:20:18,270
than simply running reinforcement learning

415
00:20:18,270 --> 00:20:21,910
inside your learn simulator

416
00:20:21,910 --> 00:20:26,590
And the way that this could work is

417
00:20:26,590 --> 00:20:30,230
you can think about your MDP, where

418
00:20:30,230 --> 00:20:33,270
you have your current state that you're in

419
00:20:33,270 --> 00:20:36,030
And then you take some action

420
00:20:36,030 --> 00:20:41,790
And then this affects your next action

421
00:20:41,790 --> 00:20:44,310
And then as a result of that, you'll

422
00:20:44,310 --> 00:20:48,270
also get some future reward

423
00:20:48,270 --> 00:20:51,980
And then this process will repeat

424
00:20:51,980 --> 00:20:55,830
You'll take another action that influences

425
00:20:55,830 --> 00:21:01,010
the state after that, which will yield some additional reward

426
00:21:01,010 --> 00:21:03,670
This reward might also depend on the action

427
00:21:03,670 --> 00:21:06,350
that you took as well

428
00:21:06,350 --> 00:21:11,670
Now, in some of the past reinforcement

429
00:21:11,670 --> 00:21:13,400
learning works that we talked about

430
00:21:13,400 --> 00:21:15,070
we kind of assumed that we didn't really

431
00:21:15,070 --> 00:21:16,970
know the relationship

432
00:21:16,970 --> 00:21:21,270
We kind of don't know how the actions affect future states

433
00:21:21,270 --> 00:21:24,110
And so we have to figure out how to optimize rewards

434
00:21:24,110 --> 00:21:28,550
without using direct gradients

435
00:21:28,550 --> 00:21:35,140
But if we can actually predict what a future state is

436
00:21:35,140 --> 00:21:37,760
and what a reward is based off of that

437
00:21:37,760 --> 00:21:40,980
if we train a reward model and a dynamics model

438
00:21:40,980 --> 00:21:42,500
then we could actually just directly

439
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:48,020
backpropagate from rewards into actions

440
00:21:48,020 --> 00:21:53,140
And so we could potentially directly pass gradients

441
00:21:53,140 --> 00:21:58,860
from your rewards into your actions

442
00:21:58,860 --> 00:22:01,060
And what this would look like is your objective

443
00:22:01,060 --> 00:22:10,660
would be to maximize either just the current action or maybe

444
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:20,780
the next sequence of actions for the sum of future rewards

445
00:22:20,780 --> 00:22:24,600
Maybe we'll do this t prime for some horizon

446
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:29,540
So maybe we're optimizing for rewards from time t

447
00:22:29,540 --> 00:22:36,580
to time t plus H

448
00:22:36,580 --> 00:22:39,460
What this would look specifically--

449
00:22:39,460 --> 00:22:42,200
I guess we can show it on the slides as well

450
00:22:42,200 --> 00:22:44,820
So we're trying to maximize the sum of rewards

451
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:49,860
And we often refer to this kind of problem

452
00:22:49,860 --> 00:22:53,700
as looking into the future is planning a sequence of actions

453
00:22:53,700 --> 00:22:56,060
that will maximize rewards

454
00:22:56,060 --> 00:23:00,700
And so if we have some model of how we predict future states

455
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:02,780
and actions and rewards and if we're

456
00:23:02,780 --> 00:23:05,060
modeling that with a neural network

457
00:23:05,060 --> 00:23:08,580
then we can also compute the gradients of that neural network

458
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:15,660
to backpropagate rewards into actions as following

459
00:23:15,660 --> 00:23:20,780
And so then, specifically, what this would look like is--

460
00:23:20,780 --> 00:23:27,810
well, first, you would collect some data

461
00:23:27,810 --> 00:23:35,610
Then you would train your model

462
00:23:35,610 --> 00:23:44,330
Maybe that's to minimize f of s, a minus the--

463
00:23:44,330 --> 00:23:45,750
this is your predicted next state

464
00:23:45,750 --> 00:23:48,490
This is your true next state

465
00:23:48,490 --> 00:23:50,490
And then once you have this model

466
00:23:50,490 --> 00:23:56,890
you could then find a set of actions that is--

467
00:23:56,890 --> 00:23:59,810
actually, just to be explicit for this optimization

468
00:23:59,810 --> 00:24:04,530
we're minimizing the parameters of our model

469
00:24:04,530 --> 00:24:12,850
And then the third step is to try to find a set of actions

470
00:24:12,850 --> 00:24:19,930
So this would be a hat t to t plus H. This is like

471
00:24:19,930 --> 00:24:23,130
what is the course of actions that will lead to good rewards

472
00:24:23,130 --> 00:24:42,280
And this would be the argmax of the sum of rewards--

473
00:24:42,280 --> 00:24:44,120
sum of t

474
00:24:44,120 --> 00:24:57,120
This is over--

475
00:24:57,120 --> 00:24:59,450
Running gradient descent on actions is a little bit--

476
00:24:59,450 --> 00:25:01,200
backpropagation to actions is a little bit

477
00:25:01,200 --> 00:25:04,560
different from the typical backprop that we look at

478
00:25:04,560 --> 00:25:06,640
So oftentimes, when we run backprop

479
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:09,320
we're doing it with regard to parameters of a neural network

480
00:25:09,320 --> 00:25:11,800
Here, we're actually backpropagating

481
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:14,400
into these actions right here

482
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:17,200
And so specifically, what this kind of third step

483
00:25:17,200 --> 00:25:28,310
would look like is you will need to basically initialize--

484
00:25:28,310 --> 00:25:30,520
just like you randomly initialize your neural network

485
00:25:30,520 --> 00:25:36,480
parameters, you'll need to initialize your actions

486
00:25:36,480 --> 00:25:40,840
Then you need to compute your gradient

487
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:53,200
So this would be the gradient of the sum of rewards with respect

488
00:25:53,200 --> 00:25:58,440
to the actions that you take

489
00:25:58,440 --> 00:26:01,040
And to compute this gradient, you

490
00:26:01,040 --> 00:26:04,000
can think of it essentially-- like if you use the chain rule

491
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:09,320
the gradient will be the sum of rewards with respect

492
00:26:09,320 --> 00:26:14,440
to the states

493
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:19,310
So that would just be based off of your reward model

494
00:26:19,310 --> 00:26:20,680
Then if you apply the chain rule

495
00:26:20,680 --> 00:26:22,780
you could then have a second term

496
00:26:22,780 --> 00:26:27,870
that's d states d actions to actually get

497
00:26:27,870 --> 00:26:37,030
the gradient of the rewards with respect to the actions

498
00:26:37,030 --> 00:26:39,290
And then once you have your gradient

499
00:26:39,290 --> 00:26:43,470
then you could use it to update your actions

500
00:26:43,470 --> 00:26:44,830
with gradient descent

501
00:26:44,830 --> 00:26:51,190
And so then maybe this is your initial a hat

502
00:26:51,190 --> 00:26:55,110
a hat t to t plus H is going to be

503
00:26:55,110 --> 00:27:06,270
your previous a hat minus alpha times this gradient right here

504
00:27:06,270 --> 00:27:11,930
I'm going to put this separately

505
00:27:11,930 --> 00:27:12,810
Yeah, great question

506
00:27:12,810 --> 00:27:14,870
Actions would be continuous in this setting

507
00:27:14,870 --> 00:27:16,830
We'll also look at scenarios where we can do it

508
00:27:16,830 --> 00:27:18,600
with discrete actions as well

509
00:27:18,600 --> 00:27:20,310
We're assuming rewards are deterministic

510
00:27:20,310 --> 00:27:21,680
But we've actually always assumed

511
00:27:21,680 --> 00:27:23,660
rewards are deterministic

512
00:27:23,660 --> 00:27:27,060
And you can consider any sort of non-determinism

513
00:27:27,060 --> 00:27:28,900
being encapsulated by the dynamics

514
00:27:28,900 --> 00:27:31,060
and in the state variable

515
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:31,720
Correct, yeah

516
00:27:31,720 --> 00:27:33,620
So here, we have a dynamics model

517
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:35,740
And I guess I didn't explicitly write this

518
00:27:35,740 --> 00:27:42,900
But you would also be trading some reward function

519
00:27:42,900 --> 00:27:46,580
Maybe this is like r psi for example

520
00:27:46,580 --> 00:27:49,740
And this would be using some of the reward learning techniques

521
00:27:49,740 --> 00:27:50,760
that we've talked about

522
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:53,940
There's also some scenarios where you might know the reward

523
00:27:53,940 --> 00:27:57,260
So it might be, for example, like forward velocity if you're

524
00:27:57,260 --> 00:28:00,720
trying to learn how to walk or some distance to some goal

525
00:28:00,720 --> 00:28:01,600
that sort of thing

526
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:05,260
And in those cases, you don't need to learn a reward function

527
00:28:05,260 --> 00:28:08,800
So the question is, if your dynamics model is stochastic

528
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:17,300
how do you backprop through it

529
00:28:17,300 --> 00:28:21,020
It kind of depends on how you're modeling the stochasticity

530
00:28:21,020 --> 00:28:24,320
So if you're using a Gaussian, for example

531
00:28:24,320 --> 00:28:26,820
there are certain tricks for differentiating through samples

532
00:28:26,820 --> 00:28:30,340
from Gaussian, whereas if you're using something like a diffusion

533
00:28:30,340 --> 00:28:33,620
model, it would look a bit different

534
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:37,300
So it is kind of model dependent

535
00:28:37,300 --> 00:28:44,460
And so far, we're talking about deterministic models

536
00:28:44,460 --> 00:28:47,140
So the question is, will this start to exploit errors

537
00:28:47,140 --> 00:28:48,250
in the reward

538
00:28:48,250 --> 00:28:50,540
So this will start to exploit both errors in the reward

539
00:28:50,540 --> 00:28:52,360
but also errors in the dynamics model

540
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:56,380
So if your dynamics model is inaccurate

541
00:28:56,380 --> 00:28:58,040
or if your reward model is inaccurate--

542
00:28:58,040 --> 00:28:59,820
if either one of them is inaccurate

543
00:28:59,820 --> 00:29:03,260
then you might get actions that aren't actually

544
00:29:03,260 --> 00:29:06,340
going to take good actions in the world

545
00:29:06,340 --> 00:29:09,180
And that's a problem

546
00:29:09,180 --> 00:29:11,880
And so we'll talk about that in a second

547
00:29:11,880 --> 00:29:13,000
So this is a good question

548
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:16,020
So I guess instead of repeating it

549
00:29:16,020 --> 00:29:18,720
I think what would be helpful is--

550
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:20,470
we aren't actually learning a policy here

551
00:29:20,470 --> 00:29:22,530
And that's essentially what you're pointing out

552
00:29:22,530 --> 00:29:32,530
And this is like becoming our "policy," quote unquote

553
00:29:32,530 --> 00:29:36,800
This is like our behavior

554
00:29:36,800 --> 00:29:38,090
Do I have a good term for this

555
00:29:38,090 --> 00:29:38,510
I guess

556
00:29:38,510 --> 00:29:39,050
I mean, yeah

557
00:29:39,050 --> 00:29:44,850
This is sort of the "policy," in quotes

558
00:29:44,850 --> 00:29:47,870
And this is what we call planning

559
00:29:47,870 --> 00:29:50,090
where at every single state, we're

560
00:29:50,090 --> 00:29:53,300
actually going to be planning forward and thinking ahead

561
00:29:53,300 --> 00:29:55,170
What are the actions I should consider that I

562
00:29:55,170 --> 00:29:58,610
think will lead to good reward

563
00:29:58,610 --> 00:30:01,010
And so we'll actually be doing this optimization process

564
00:30:01,010 --> 00:30:06,850
every single state when we're doing planning

565
00:30:06,850 --> 00:30:09,890
So on the slide, we're going to be

566
00:30:09,890 --> 00:30:12,610
running some policy to collect data

567
00:30:12,610 --> 00:30:17,090
learning a model to try to be able to accurately model

568
00:30:17,090 --> 00:30:20,410
the dynamics, potentially also learning a reward function as

569
00:30:20,410 --> 00:30:22,370
well

570
00:30:22,370 --> 00:30:23,970
And then we'll be backpropagating

571
00:30:23,970 --> 00:30:27,490
through the reward and the dynamics

572
00:30:27,490 --> 00:30:31,770
in order to choose actions

573
00:30:31,770 --> 00:30:33,850
And then once we choose those actions

574
00:30:33,850 --> 00:30:37,730
we'll be then be executing these actions in the environment

575
00:30:37,730 --> 00:30:40,570
And then after we execute those actions

576
00:30:40,570 --> 00:30:44,290
we'll then be rerunning this planning process again

577
00:30:44,290 --> 00:30:48,290
So in particular, I guess maybe to explicitly write that out

578
00:30:48,290 --> 00:30:50,970
we'll have a step 4 here

579
00:30:50,970 --> 00:31:00,450
That is to actually execute a hat t to t plus H

580
00:31:00,450 --> 00:31:08,770
And then you will then move on back to step 3 after you've

581
00:31:08,770 --> 00:31:12,250
executed these H actions

582
00:31:12,250 --> 00:31:17,070
So the question is, we're only planning over a horizon of H

583
00:31:17,070 --> 00:31:23,760
And what if we have an infinite horizon

584
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:27,020
In principle, the math--

585
00:31:27,020 --> 00:31:33,040
well, it's a good question

586
00:31:33,040 --> 00:31:35,020
So we'll talk a little bit about this

587
00:31:35,020 --> 00:31:37,600
For now, we're going to be only considering this short horizon

588
00:31:37,600 --> 00:31:40,000
planning

589
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,940
And in eight-ish slides or something

590
00:31:42,940 --> 00:31:44,520
I'll talk a little bit more about what

591
00:31:44,520 --> 00:31:46,480
happens if you want to do infinite or very long

592
00:31:46,480 --> 00:31:47,760
horizon things

593
00:31:47,760 --> 00:31:56,800
And we'll also talk about some of the shortcomings of this

594
00:31:56,800 --> 00:32:01,080
Now, so far, we talked about gradient descent

595
00:32:01,080 --> 00:32:03,680
for optimizing actions

596
00:32:03,680 --> 00:32:06,120
And this is actually not the only way

597
00:32:06,120 --> 00:32:07,660
that you can do optimization

598
00:32:07,660 --> 00:32:09,520
So it's probably the optimization technique

599
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:12,000
that you're most familiar with because we use it all

600
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:13,560
the time for neural networks

601
00:32:13,560 --> 00:32:17,510
But there's other ways that we could optimize a function

602
00:32:17,510 --> 00:32:21,750
specifically when we're trying to optimize the sum of rewards

603
00:32:21,750 --> 00:32:25,390
And if we think about gradient-based optimization

604
00:32:25,390 --> 00:32:28,770
typically we have something that we're trying to optimize over

605
00:32:28,770 --> 00:32:32,650
In this case, we're trying to be optimizing over actions

606
00:32:32,650 --> 00:32:35,350
And the thing that we're trying to optimize

607
00:32:35,350 --> 00:32:44,060
is the sum of rewards maybe

608
00:32:44,060 --> 00:32:45,930
Actually, also, there's a small mistake here

609
00:32:45,930 --> 00:32:48,100
This should be a plus sign because we're maximizing

610
00:32:48,100 --> 00:32:50,330
not minimizing

611
00:32:50,330 --> 00:32:52,910
So the way that gradient descent works is you kind of randomly

612
00:32:52,910 --> 00:32:56,070
initialize some action, like right here

613
00:32:56,070 --> 00:32:58,930
And then you evaluate the gradient at that position

614
00:32:58,930 --> 00:33:01,030
So maybe you find that the gradient

615
00:33:01,030 --> 00:33:04,790
is like pointed this way

616
00:33:04,790 --> 00:33:09,470
And then you then say, OK, I should move probably over here

617
00:33:09,470 --> 00:33:11,510
And then maybe you sample this

618
00:33:11,510 --> 00:33:14,130
You choose this action because that's kind of a step size away

619
00:33:14,130 --> 00:33:16,110
You evaluate the gradient there

620
00:33:16,110 --> 00:33:19,870
And you find that the gradient maybe looks something like this

621
00:33:19,870 --> 00:33:21,950
And then you say, OK, maybe I should move further

622
00:33:21,950 --> 00:33:23,330
to the left but a little bit less

623
00:33:23,330 --> 00:33:25,710
because the slope is less steep

624
00:33:25,710 --> 00:33:28,910
And then you get maybe something that looks like this

625
00:33:28,910 --> 00:33:31,330
And you're like, OK, the gradient is 0

626
00:33:31,330 --> 00:33:34,890
And so maybe I won't be sampling any further actions

627
00:33:34,890 --> 00:33:38,590
And then your final choice after running gradient descent

628
00:33:38,590 --> 00:33:41,990
is this value right here

629
00:33:41,990 --> 00:33:46,990
Now, an alternative way to try to do a kind of optimization

630
00:33:46,990 --> 00:33:50,650
is instead what I'll call sampling-based optimization

631
00:33:50,650 --> 00:33:54,270
This is also called gradient-free optimization

632
00:33:54,270 --> 00:33:58,030
And the way that this works is if you have some set of actions

633
00:33:58,030 --> 00:34:04,350
and some sum of rewards, instead of just starting from one place

634
00:34:04,350 --> 00:34:06,190
and evaluating the gradient, we're

635
00:34:06,190 --> 00:34:08,510
actually not going to use the gradients at all

636
00:34:08,510 --> 00:34:10,630
And we'll just sample a bunch of actions

637
00:34:10,630 --> 00:34:13,060
and see what the values are

638
00:34:13,060 --> 00:34:15,900
So maybe we'll, for example, a sample like this action

639
00:34:15,900 --> 00:34:22,620
We'll sample this action maybe sample this one, maybe sample

640
00:34:22,620 --> 00:34:26,219
this one, and so forth

641
00:34:26,219 --> 00:34:31,060
And the first version of a sampling-based method

642
00:34:31,060 --> 00:34:35,060
could just be to sample a bunch of things in parallel

643
00:34:35,060 --> 00:34:38,139
and then pick the best one

644
00:34:38,139 --> 00:34:41,940
This is basically just called random sampling

645
00:34:41,940 --> 00:34:44,739
Sometimes, it actually works better

646
00:34:44,739 --> 00:34:46,000
than you think it might do

647
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:47,420
And if we were to do that, then we

648
00:34:47,420 --> 00:34:50,239
would say that, OK, this action has the highest

649
00:34:50,239 --> 00:34:54,940
And so maybe I will return the corresponding action

650
00:34:54,940 --> 00:34:57,090
for this value

651
00:34:57,090 --> 00:34:59,460
But you can also do something a bit better than that even

652
00:34:59,460 --> 00:35:01,590
without gradient optimization, which

653
00:35:01,590 --> 00:35:03,220
is that you could look at these samples

654
00:35:03,220 --> 00:35:05,640
and say, OK, it looks like--

655
00:35:05,640 --> 00:35:09,700
maybe we have one additional sample thing that we've--

656
00:35:09,700 --> 00:35:13,250
maybe we've also sampled something like this

657
00:35:13,250 --> 00:35:15,650
And in that case, one thing that we could do

658
00:35:15,650 --> 00:35:19,050
is instead of just considering this one

659
00:35:19,050 --> 00:35:21,570
and taking the best is we could say, maybe we should sample

660
00:35:21,570 --> 00:35:26,450
more over here, because these are the ones that were the best

661
00:35:26,450 --> 00:35:31,490
And so we could basically try to sample

662
00:35:31,490 --> 00:35:32,990
more from the things that were good

663
00:35:32,990 --> 00:35:34,450
So we could take the ones that were

664
00:35:34,450 --> 00:35:37,330
the best fit, some kind of distribution over those

665
00:35:37,330 --> 00:35:39,790
like a simple distribution, like a Gaussian

666
00:35:39,790 --> 00:35:40,330
Say that, OK

667
00:35:40,330 --> 00:35:42,080
These were the actions that were the best

668
00:35:42,080 --> 00:35:44,250
So maybe we could fit some Gaussian or sample

669
00:35:44,250 --> 00:35:47,490
more from the actions here and then do

670
00:35:47,490 --> 00:35:51,290
another round of sampling from that new distribution, where

671
00:35:51,290 --> 00:35:53,790
now you're going to be sampling more from this distribution

672
00:35:53,790 --> 00:35:57,010
You find that when you sample for a second round, maybe

673
00:35:57,010 --> 00:36:01,890
you get samples that look like this

674
00:36:01,890 --> 00:36:03,570
And then that allows you to hone in

675
00:36:03,570 --> 00:36:11,170
on what a good action might be

676
00:36:11,170 --> 00:36:16,370
So on the slides, instead of with actions

677
00:36:16,370 --> 00:36:19,450
and maximizing rewards, I did it with parameters and minimizing

678
00:36:19,450 --> 00:36:20,690
some loss function

679
00:36:20,690 --> 00:36:22,850
But all the ideas are the same

680
00:36:22,850 --> 00:36:27,950
So for gradient descent, we pick an initial value

681
00:36:27,950 --> 00:36:30,910
evaluate the gradient, move in the direction of the gradient

682
00:36:30,910 --> 00:36:32,590
and repeat that process

683
00:36:32,590 --> 00:36:36,930
There's some underlying function that we don't know

684
00:36:36,930 --> 00:36:40,930
And so we're trying to get to the minimum of that function

685
00:36:40,930 --> 00:36:43,090
using these gradients

686
00:36:43,090 --> 00:36:45,770
And likewise with sampling, or another way to call this is

687
00:36:45,770 --> 00:36:49,010
a 0th order optimization, we're instead going to be sampling

688
00:36:49,010 --> 00:36:55,090
points and trying to figure out what a good value is just from

689
00:36:55,090 --> 00:36:56,010
sampling

690
00:36:56,010 --> 00:36:57,490
And again, there is some underlying

691
00:36:57,490 --> 00:36:59,630
curve here that we don't know

692
00:36:59,630 --> 00:37:04,170
We can only evaluate samples or, in the case of gradient descent

693
00:37:04,170 --> 00:37:06,850
the gradient at those samples

694
00:37:06,850 --> 00:37:09,440
One other nice thing about a sampling-based optimization

695
00:37:09,440 --> 00:37:11,600
is that it means that you don't need gradients

696
00:37:11,600 --> 00:37:14,580
So it can apply to discrete variables

697
00:37:14,580 --> 00:37:18,240
It can also apply to scenarios where you maybe

698
00:37:18,240 --> 00:37:20,160
know the reward function

699
00:37:20,160 --> 00:37:21,500
You have reward values

700
00:37:21,500 --> 00:37:24,000
But you don't know what the gradient of the reward function

701
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:29,980
is

702
00:37:29,980 --> 00:37:31,860
If it's a discrete reward function

703
00:37:31,860 --> 00:37:35,480
then getting the gradient might be challenging

704
00:37:35,480 --> 00:37:39,120
And then to formalize this kind of iterative sampling

705
00:37:39,120 --> 00:37:41,000
process, where we're trying to sample more

706
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:44,920
from the good actions, one method for doing this

707
00:37:44,920 --> 00:37:46,920
is cross-entropy method

708
00:37:46,920 --> 00:37:49,860
There's also a way to do this with-- well

709
00:37:49,860 --> 00:37:53,240
if you have discrete actions, you could also just--

710
00:37:53,240 --> 00:37:55,620
well, there's somewhat of a different process

711
00:37:55,620 --> 00:37:57,480
But you could also just use random sampling

712
00:37:57,480 --> 00:38:00,600
if your action space is small enough

713
00:38:00,600 --> 00:38:03,040
So what cross-entropy method, not

714
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:04,880
to be confused with the cross-entropy loss

715
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:07,180
is you sample from some initial distribution

716
00:38:07,180 --> 00:38:10,000
This could be a uniform distribution

717
00:38:10,000 --> 00:38:16,080
Then rank the samples according to their loss

718
00:38:16,080 --> 00:38:18,000
Like, this would be the best one

719
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:20,460
This would be the second best, the third best, fourth best

720
00:38:20,460 --> 00:38:22,080
and so forth

721
00:38:22,080 --> 00:38:25,320
And then take the best ones like, these three ones

722
00:38:25,320 --> 00:38:29,920
And then fit a distribution, like a Gaussian distribution

723
00:38:29,920 --> 00:38:33,000
to those best samples

724
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:36,560
And then resample from your distribution

725
00:38:36,560 --> 00:38:39,920
rerank, and repeat this process

726
00:38:39,920 --> 00:38:43,840
So it's basically like a very simple sampling-based way

727
00:38:43,840 --> 00:38:47,480
to try to get, in this case, good actions

728
00:38:47,480 --> 00:38:50,000
It's like a general optimization technique

729
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:56,480
that can be used for any sort of objective

730
00:38:56,480 --> 00:38:56,980
Cool

731
00:38:56,980 --> 00:38:59,640
And then after this process, eventually

732
00:38:59,640 --> 00:39:01,380
after you run some number of iterations

733
00:39:01,380 --> 00:39:04,160
you'll return the best sample that you

734
00:39:04,160 --> 00:39:06,800
found in the last iteration

735
00:39:06,800 --> 00:39:08,550
The question is, can you also use sampling

736
00:39:08,550 --> 00:39:10,910
to approximate gradients

737
00:39:10,910 --> 00:39:11,470
Yes

738
00:39:11,470 --> 00:39:15,310
So there's something called finite differences, where

739
00:39:15,310 --> 00:39:17,250
if you don't have a way to get gradients

740
00:39:17,250 --> 00:39:19,310
then you could try to sample basically

741
00:39:19,310 --> 00:39:23,030
points very close together and then use that to basically look

742
00:39:23,030 --> 00:39:27,230
at the difference between this point and this point and use

743
00:39:27,230 --> 00:39:28,870
that to estimate the gradient

744
00:39:28,870 --> 00:39:31,490
There's pros and cons to it

745
00:39:31,490 --> 00:39:34,270
But there's definitely a valid approach

746
00:39:34,270 --> 00:39:34,910
Yeah

747
00:39:34,910 --> 00:39:37,330
The question is, why do I put the quotes around the policy

748
00:39:37,330 --> 00:39:38,430
here

749
00:39:38,430 --> 00:39:42,610
The reason is that we're not learning a parametric policy

750
00:39:42,610 --> 00:39:45,990
We're not learning a neural network to represent a policy

751
00:39:45,990 --> 00:39:50,230
And there's actually an optimization process

752
00:39:50,230 --> 00:39:52,950
underlying the policy here

753
00:39:52,950 --> 00:39:57,670
And so typically, it's a policy in the sense

754
00:39:57,670 --> 00:40:01,270
that it is mapping from a state to an action or a state

755
00:40:01,270 --> 00:40:04,710
to actually multiple future actions

756
00:40:04,710 --> 00:40:09,130
But it's often not referred to a policy

757
00:40:09,130 --> 00:40:10,500
to differentiate it from policies

758
00:40:10,500 --> 00:40:13,820
that are more like neural network

759
00:40:13,820 --> 00:40:16,740
like that are more compact

760
00:40:16,740 --> 00:40:19,300
Well, you can also have a linear function that's

761
00:40:19,300 --> 00:40:20,740
mapping states to actions

762
00:40:20,740 --> 00:40:22,580
The main thing is that it's a process that's

763
00:40:22,580 --> 00:40:24,480
like an iterative optimization process

764
00:40:24,480 --> 00:40:27,140
rather than some sort of closed form, like here's

765
00:40:27,140 --> 00:40:28,730
an action for a state

766
00:40:28,730 --> 00:40:30,980
So the question is, can we talk a little bit about how

767
00:40:30,980 --> 00:40:32,700
this policy is stored

768
00:40:32,700 --> 00:40:39,860
So the only thing that we have learned at this point

769
00:40:39,860 --> 00:40:43,380
is a dynamics model and potentially a reward function

770
00:40:43,380 --> 00:40:45,580
And so the only thing that we have stored

771
00:40:45,580 --> 00:40:49,100
is the parameters of the model and the parameters

772
00:40:49,100 --> 00:40:51,740
of your reward model

773
00:40:51,740 --> 00:40:56,140
And then when you're actually trying to figure out what to do

774
00:40:56,140 --> 00:40:59,420
and what actions to take, what policy to follow

775
00:40:59,420 --> 00:41:01,420
we don't have a neural network that's telling us

776
00:41:01,420 --> 00:41:02,300
what actions to take

777
00:41:02,300 --> 00:41:04,880
Instead, every single time step, you're thinking, OK

778
00:41:04,880 --> 00:41:07,820
what's going to happen if I do this, this, or this

779
00:41:07,820 --> 00:41:09,980
And then choosing actions that you think

780
00:41:09,980 --> 00:41:15,060
are good as a result of that kind of thinking process

781
00:41:15,060 --> 00:41:17,920
You're using a lot more compute as part of this process

782
00:41:17,920 --> 00:41:19,900
And you're doing that in real time

783
00:41:19,900 --> 00:41:22,980
as you're trying to figure out what actions to take

784
00:41:22,980 --> 00:41:30,260
Yeah, we're not recording what we're doing right now

785
00:41:30,260 --> 00:41:34,420
So the question is, when we sample actions, can some of them

786
00:41:34,420 --> 00:41:35,480
be out of distribution

787
00:41:35,480 --> 00:41:40,700
So yeah, when you select the distribution to sample from

788
00:41:40,700 --> 00:41:42,900
your actions might be out of distribution

789
00:41:42,900 --> 00:41:45,300
compared to the data that the model was trained on

790
00:41:45,300 --> 00:41:50,620
And we'll talk in the next slide on how to deal with that

791
00:41:50,620 --> 00:41:52,260
So the question is, because we're

792
00:41:52,260 --> 00:41:54,220
doing so much compute at test time

793
00:41:54,220 --> 00:41:57,340
if you've finished computing and by the time you finish

794
00:41:57,340 --> 00:41:59,000
computing, the state has changed

795
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:05,070
how do you account for that

796
00:42:05,070 --> 00:42:06,990
I don't know if I have a great answer for you

797
00:42:06,990 --> 00:42:09,790
I think that, typically, when people run this in practice

798
00:42:09,790 --> 00:42:13,490
they just try to make that compute process as fast

799
00:42:13,490 --> 00:42:18,710
as possible so that they don't have to account for the change

800
00:42:18,710 --> 00:42:25,330
I guess one thing you can do is you're kind of planning actually

801
00:42:25,330 --> 00:42:26,750
a sequence of actions here

802
00:42:26,750 --> 00:42:33,850
And so if you can estimate how long that computation took--

803
00:42:33,850 --> 00:42:36,490
if it took like two time steps, for example, then instead

804
00:42:36,490 --> 00:42:38,490
of starting by executing at, you could actually

805
00:42:38,490 --> 00:42:42,330
start by executing at plus 3 or at plus 2

806
00:42:42,330 --> 00:42:45,410
depending on how [? that ?] one works

807
00:42:45,410 --> 00:42:47,690
And so that's kind of accounting for the fact

808
00:42:47,690 --> 00:42:50,650
that time has moved forward

809
00:42:50,650 --> 00:42:53,850
The downside there is that at and at

810
00:42:53,850 --> 00:42:56,300
plus 1 might be different from what was actually executed

811
00:42:56,300 --> 00:42:57,970
And so you might be in a different state

812
00:42:57,970 --> 00:43:00,130
from where you thought you ended up in

813
00:43:00,130 --> 00:43:02,860
And so that introduces challenges

814
00:43:02,860 --> 00:43:05,600
And it's actually somewhat of an open research problem

815
00:43:05,600 --> 00:43:12,420
on how best to handle that sort of thing

816
00:43:12,420 --> 00:43:17,280
especially if you're basically asynchronously like running

817
00:43:17,280 --> 00:43:18,040
this process

818
00:43:18,040 --> 00:43:27,760
And also, the world is evolving as well

819
00:43:27,760 --> 00:43:30,480
So going back to doing this with gradient descent

820
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:34,280
versus with samples, there's pros and cons

821
00:43:34,280 --> 00:43:36,020
to gradient-based and sampling-based

822
00:43:36,020 --> 00:43:38,520
And this helps elucidate why sampling-based actually can

823
00:43:38,520 --> 00:43:41,200
be a good choice for planning

824
00:43:41,200 --> 00:43:44,960
So gradient descent is very scalable even

825
00:43:44,960 --> 00:43:47,120
to very high dimensions

826
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:50,490
And it works well, especially in overparameterized regimes

827
00:43:50,490 --> 00:43:52,200
when you have a big neural network that's

828
00:43:52,200 --> 00:43:54,080
representing a function that may actually

829
00:43:54,080 --> 00:43:57,840
have fewer free parameters

830
00:43:57,840 --> 00:44:00,400
The downside is that it requires a nice optimization

831
00:44:00,400 --> 00:44:06,560
landscape where your gradients are fairly smooth rather

832
00:44:06,560 --> 00:44:09,480
than not smooth, I guess

833
00:44:09,480 --> 00:44:11,580
With neural networks, it ends up being--

834
00:44:11,580 --> 00:44:13,000
especially because neural networks

835
00:44:13,000 --> 00:44:14,760
have high dimensional parameter spaces

836
00:44:14,760 --> 00:44:17,440
and are overparameterized, the pros

837
00:44:17,440 --> 00:44:20,280
end up heavily outweighing the cons here

838
00:44:20,280 --> 00:44:22,360
Some of the upsides with sampling-based approaches

839
00:44:22,360 --> 00:44:24,800
is that it's really easy to parallelize because you

840
00:44:24,800 --> 00:44:28,800
can actually estimate multiple samples

841
00:44:28,800 --> 00:44:30,800
in parallel, whereas gradient descent

842
00:44:30,800 --> 00:44:33,500
it inevitably has to be a very sequential process, where

843
00:44:33,500 --> 00:44:35,960
you compute one gradient and then apply it and then compute

844
00:44:35,960 --> 00:44:37,560
the next gradient and so forth

845
00:44:37,560 --> 00:44:40,020
And this parallelization can actually, in some cases

846
00:44:40,020 --> 00:44:42,080
make it faster

847
00:44:42,080 --> 00:44:45,320
It doesn't require gradients if you don't have them

848
00:44:45,320 --> 00:44:48,580
The downside is that it scales poorly to high dimensions

849
00:44:48,580 --> 00:44:50,780
Because if you have a really high dimensional space

850
00:44:50,780 --> 00:44:53,680
it's very hard to cover that high dimensional space

851
00:44:53,680 --> 00:44:55,920
with lots of different samples

852
00:44:55,920 --> 00:44:58,150
So in one dimensions, something like this

853
00:44:58,150 --> 00:45:00,350
can actually work really, really well

854
00:45:00,350 --> 00:45:03,070
But if you can imagine, instead of this being one-dimensional

855
00:45:03,070 --> 00:45:06,630
or two-dimensional or 10-dimensional

856
00:45:06,630 --> 00:45:09,630
you have to have your samples cover that space well in order

857
00:45:09,630 --> 00:45:17,070
to find a good optimum

858
00:45:17,070 --> 00:45:19,990
So specifically, what the planning approach

859
00:45:19,990 --> 00:45:25,310
would look like with a sampling-based optimization is

860
00:45:25,310 --> 00:45:30,550
you will still run some policy and train some model, like we

861
00:45:30,550 --> 00:45:32,210
did in the first two steps

862
00:45:32,210 --> 00:45:35,630
And we'll also be choosing actions that

863
00:45:35,630 --> 00:45:37,950
are trying to maximize rewards

864
00:45:37,950 --> 00:45:41,310
So essentially, things are the same

865
00:45:41,310 --> 00:45:45,790
except the way that we do this optimization problem is going

866
00:45:45,790 --> 00:45:51,190
to change, where instead of running gradient descent

867
00:45:51,190 --> 00:45:54,030
we could instead sample a bunch of different actions

868
00:45:54,030 --> 00:45:56,110
imagine what will happen in the future

869
00:45:56,110 --> 00:45:59,350
and what rewards we'll get, and then

870
00:45:59,350 --> 00:46:03,390
potentially refine our selection of actions

871
00:46:03,390 --> 00:46:10,270
iteratively to then get a good outcome

872
00:46:10,270 --> 00:46:12,910
And so in terms of what this looks

873
00:46:12,910 --> 00:46:17,070
like, if we use capital A as shorthand to denote

874
00:46:17,070 --> 00:46:24,710
this at to at plus H, then version 1 is if we just

875
00:46:24,710 --> 00:46:27,030
do one round of sampling, then we'll

876
00:46:27,030 --> 00:46:30,470
sample a large number of possible action choices

877
00:46:30,470 --> 00:46:34,110
we could take from some distribution

878
00:46:34,110 --> 00:46:41,750
then choose the set of actions that had the highest reward

879
00:46:41,750 --> 00:46:47,390
and then run those actions in the environment

880
00:46:47,390 --> 00:46:49,850
The technical name for this is often called random shooting

881
00:46:49,850 --> 00:46:52,430
because you're kind of shooting out different possible samples

882
00:46:52,430 --> 00:46:55,020
of what could happen in the future, imagining the future

883
00:46:55,020 --> 00:47:00,430
evaluating the reward, and then just picking the one shot that

884
00:47:00,430 --> 00:47:04,020
had the highest reward

885
00:47:04,020 --> 00:47:08,820
Now, the downside and the thing that's

886
00:47:08,820 --> 00:47:11,780
kind of unsatisfying about this first algorithm

887
00:47:11,780 --> 00:47:15,260
is that this distribution may not be very good

888
00:47:15,260 --> 00:47:17,930
And so what we could do is we could

889
00:47:17,930 --> 00:47:20,140
use the cross-entropy method, where we're iteratively

890
00:47:20,140 --> 00:47:22,340
improving that distribution, where we start

891
00:47:22,340 --> 00:47:25,580
from some distribution, maybe a uniform distribution

892
00:47:25,580 --> 00:47:29,940
evaluate, rank, and pick the best samples that

893
00:47:29,940 --> 00:47:33,340
have the highest rewards and then

894
00:47:33,340 --> 00:47:36,660
refit a new distribution to those best samples

895
00:47:36,660 --> 00:47:39,580
and repeat that process, where we're then

896
00:47:39,580 --> 00:47:43,740
going to be sampling from that better distribution

897
00:47:43,740 --> 00:47:49,740
and then evaluating those samples and repeating

898
00:47:49,740 --> 00:47:51,580
If this is parallelized, this process

899
00:47:51,580 --> 00:47:53,660
actually can be quite fast

900
00:47:53,660 --> 00:47:56,630
If you have low-dimensional actions

901
00:47:56,630 --> 00:48:00,050
it's actually also pretty simple to implement

902
00:48:00,050 --> 00:48:02,010
But like we talked about, it doesn't scale well

903
00:48:02,010 --> 00:48:03,730
to high dimensions

904
00:48:03,730 --> 00:48:08,650
And so it's best if you have a somewhat low-dimensional space

905
00:48:08,650 --> 00:48:09,830
that you're considering

906
00:48:09,830 --> 00:48:13,050
This includes both H and a because the optimization

907
00:48:13,050 --> 00:48:17,570
that you're considering is over this vector right here

908
00:48:17,570 --> 00:48:22,010
And the size of this is the dimensionality of your action

909
00:48:22,010 --> 00:48:32,970
space times the horizon

910
00:48:32,970 --> 00:48:37,430
Let's run through an example for roughly how this would work

911
00:48:37,430 --> 00:48:43,010
So let's say that we are in this simple, beautiful environment

912
00:48:43,010 --> 00:48:46,830
And you are trying to maximize reward

913
00:48:46,830 --> 00:48:49,370
Maybe you're trying to get to the highest point

914
00:48:49,370 --> 00:48:52,850
Because the highest point, you get the nicest view

915
00:48:52,850 --> 00:48:56,410
So your reward is just like the height of where you get to

916
00:48:56,410 --> 00:48:57,510
You run some policy

917
00:48:57,510 --> 00:48:59,850
You collect some data like this

918
00:48:59,850 --> 00:49:04,890
And maybe you learn that when you train a model on this data

919
00:49:04,890 --> 00:49:08,720
that when you move right, then you go higher

920
00:49:08,720 --> 00:49:10,350
Because generally, when you move right

921
00:49:10,350 --> 00:49:14,090
the elevation became higher

922
00:49:14,090 --> 00:49:20,890
And then when you try to choose actions according to your model

923
00:49:20,890 --> 00:49:23,130
then you're going to learn that you should go right

924
00:49:23,130 --> 00:49:26,130
So you'll learn that when you're actually

925
00:49:26,130 --> 00:49:29,460
kind of finding actions that maximize reward

926
00:49:29,460 --> 00:49:35,370
your set of actions will be to go right

927
00:49:35,370 --> 00:49:39,370
And then you'll actually execute those actions

928
00:49:39,370 --> 00:49:42,360
And you'll go right, which is good for a bit

929
00:49:42,360 --> 00:49:44,030
You'll be maximizing reward for a while

930
00:49:44,030 --> 00:49:47,930
And then at some point, you'll fall off the cliff

931
00:49:47,930 --> 00:49:51,960
And that's not so good

932
00:49:51,960 --> 00:49:54,020
And this is because the thing that we talked

933
00:49:54,020 --> 00:49:55,520
about at the very beginning, there's

934
00:49:55,520 --> 00:49:58,720
this data distribution mismatch where

935
00:49:58,720 --> 00:50:01,280
the data that you collected at the beginning

936
00:50:01,280 --> 00:50:04,600
doesn't cover the data that you are

937
00:50:04,600 --> 00:50:08,560
taking with your final policy

938
00:50:08,560 --> 00:50:11,560
And so we have a simple fix to this

939
00:50:11,560 --> 00:50:15,400
The simple fix is that when we then execute actions

940
00:50:15,400 --> 00:50:20,280
in our environment, we can then add those actions to our data

941
00:50:20,280 --> 00:50:24,180
set and refit our model to that data set

942
00:50:24,180 --> 00:50:28,160
learn a more accurate model, and repeat this process

943
00:50:28,160 --> 00:50:30,840
So then this is starting to look a little bit more like

944
00:50:30,840 --> 00:50:33,960
a reinforcement learning algorithm because we're actually

945
00:50:33,960 --> 00:50:36,960
iteratively collecting data, fitting a better model--

946
00:50:36,960 --> 00:50:40,400
in this case, a dynamics model--

947
00:50:40,400 --> 00:50:42,920
sampling actions, choosing actions to execute

948
00:50:42,920 --> 00:50:47,600
and running those actions in the environment

949
00:50:47,600 --> 00:50:51,270
And so then when we do this, we will then execute the actions

950
00:50:51,270 --> 00:50:52,130
that we planned

951
00:50:52,130 --> 00:50:53,370
So we'll go off the cliff

952
00:50:53,370 --> 00:50:56,170
Then we'll learn that our model will become more accurate

953
00:50:56,170 --> 00:50:58,770
We'll learn that if we go right past this point

954
00:50:58,770 --> 00:51:01,010
we get a lot of negative rewards

955
00:51:01,010 --> 00:51:05,230
Or we get a lot of very low elevation, which corresponds

956
00:51:05,230 --> 00:51:06,550
to negative rewards

957
00:51:06,550 --> 00:51:10,870
And then we'll be able to learn a model that goes to the top

958
00:51:10,870 --> 00:51:17,350
and stops

959
00:51:17,350 --> 00:51:21,070
Now, there is a number of things that we could still

960
00:51:21,070 --> 00:51:24,150
improve about this algorithm

961
00:51:24,150 --> 00:51:26,990
And let's talk about a few of those

962
00:51:26,990 --> 00:51:35,950
So, so far, we've been executing this set of actions

963
00:51:35,950 --> 00:51:38,010
right here in the environment

964
00:51:38,010 --> 00:51:40,510
So we've been planning out a sequence of actions

965
00:51:40,510 --> 00:51:41,330
executing them

966
00:51:41,330 --> 00:51:50,750
And so what that looks like is we have our agent

967
00:51:50,750 --> 00:51:56,310
And our agent is like--

968
00:51:56,310 --> 00:51:59,990
we're running H actions in the environment--

969
00:51:59,990 --> 00:52:04,030
so action at, at plus 1, at plus 2

970
00:52:04,030 --> 00:52:08,750
and so forth in some environment

971
00:52:08,750 --> 00:52:10,270
And we're executing those actions

972
00:52:10,270 --> 00:52:15,510
without actually having access to the previous intermediate

973
00:52:15,510 --> 00:52:17,710
states

974
00:52:17,710 --> 00:52:22,230
And one alternative that you can consider

975
00:52:22,230 --> 00:52:25,270
is something where at each time, we're

976
00:52:25,270 --> 00:52:29,030
going to be planning at to at plus H. But we could also

977
00:52:29,030 --> 00:52:37,670
have something where we plan at to t plus H

978
00:52:37,670 --> 00:52:43,790
But we only actually execute at in the environment

979
00:52:43,790 --> 00:52:50,540
And then we then get the next state back in return

980
00:52:50,540 --> 00:52:56,220
And then we replan at the next time step a new set of actions

981
00:52:56,220 --> 00:52:59,180
And so the difference between these two things--

982
00:52:59,180 --> 00:53:04,860
this is often what's called open loop because you're not actually

983
00:53:04,860 --> 00:53:08,640
using the feedback from the environment

984
00:53:08,640 --> 00:53:15,300
the intermediate st's, when you are executing future actions

985
00:53:15,300 --> 00:53:19,100
whereas this is often called closed loop

986
00:53:19,100 --> 00:53:22,700
because you're actually kind of replanning

987
00:53:22,700 --> 00:53:28,220
as you get new states

988
00:53:28,220 --> 00:53:30,700
So here's what that looks like

989
00:53:30,700 --> 00:53:38,420
Now, I'm curious, does anyone have thoughts on scenarios

990
00:53:38,420 --> 00:53:43,060
in which it might be bad to be open loop like this

991
00:53:43,060 --> 00:53:49,460
where you're only planning based off of st and not planning based

992
00:53:49,460 --> 00:53:53,160
off of all of the future st's

993
00:53:53,160 --> 00:53:55,160
Yeah, so when the environment is changing a lot

994
00:53:55,160 --> 00:53:57,140
So if the environment changed in a way that

995
00:53:57,140 --> 00:54:00,020
is different from what you anticipated because you maybe

996
00:54:00,020 --> 00:54:02,420
weren't able to predict everything that would happen

997
00:54:02,420 --> 00:54:05,340
then you won't react to those changes

998
00:54:05,340 --> 00:54:08,580
when you act in an open loop way

999
00:54:08,580 --> 00:54:10,580
And instead, if you're closed loop

1000
00:54:10,580 --> 00:54:13,980
and you're reconsidering based off of the latest states

1001
00:54:13,980 --> 00:54:18,180
then you're able to actually react to those changes

1002
00:54:18,180 --> 00:54:22,500
and, potentially in some cases, actually

1003
00:54:22,500 --> 00:54:25,860
have a much, much better policy, whereas in scenarios

1004
00:54:25,860 --> 00:54:28,680
where the world is a little bit more deterministic

1005
00:54:28,680 --> 00:54:33,160
then it can be OK to be a little bit open loop at times

1006
00:54:33,160 --> 00:54:36,400
But you usually never want to have at be too large

1007
00:54:36,400 --> 00:54:41,860
Otherwise, you're executing two open loops

1008
00:54:41,860 --> 00:54:45,650
Now, we talked about how we can make planning closed loop, which

1009
00:54:45,650 --> 00:54:52,750
is instead of executing the entire action sequence

1010
00:54:52,750 --> 00:54:55,150
you just execute only the first planned action

1011
00:54:55,150 --> 00:54:58,130
So we're still going to plan a sequence of actions

1012
00:54:58,130 --> 00:55:00,790
because we need to consider not just the first action

1013
00:55:00,790 --> 00:55:03,430
but how all of our actions are going to affect future rewards

1014
00:55:03,430 --> 00:55:05,450
But then we'll execute only the first one

1015
00:55:05,450 --> 00:55:07,530
observe the resulting state

1016
00:55:07,530 --> 00:55:12,890
And then we will then replan an action sequence starting

1017
00:55:12,890 --> 00:55:18,890
at that new next state

1018
00:55:18,890 --> 00:55:23,370
And then we can also, like before, kind of also

1019
00:55:23,370 --> 00:55:28,310
add the data to our data set and periodically refit our model

1020
00:55:28,310 --> 00:55:32,220
based off of the latest data

1021
00:55:32,220 --> 00:55:34,090
This algorithm right here is something called

1022
00:55:34,090 --> 00:55:36,250
model predictive control

1023
00:55:36,250 --> 00:55:42,130
It's very, very common in certain robotic systems

1024
00:55:42,130 --> 00:55:43,580
very widely used

1025
00:55:43,580 --> 00:55:49,360
It's also used in other kind of simple control systems

1026
00:55:49,360 --> 00:55:56,120
as well, where you can model lower dimensional systems

1027
00:55:56,120 --> 00:55:58,360
It can be quite effective for legged robots

1028
00:55:58,360 --> 00:56:03,160
as well, where you're trying to learn models

1029
00:56:03,160 --> 00:56:08,360
And you're able to plan over the next couple seconds

1030
00:56:08,360 --> 00:56:14,000
to figure out what will lead to good performance

1031
00:56:14,000 --> 00:56:18,400
over the next couple of seconds

1032
00:56:18,400 --> 00:56:23,240
And this sort of replanning, when you actually

1033
00:56:23,240 --> 00:56:25,580
are considering a new plan at the next time step

1034
00:56:25,580 --> 00:56:28,000
if something changes or if you made a mistake, you slipped

1035
00:56:28,000 --> 00:56:29,720
or something, this replanning allows

1036
00:56:29,720 --> 00:56:31,970
you to actually consider a completely different course

1037
00:56:31,970 --> 00:56:39,920
of action and, for example, correct for not

1038
00:56:39,920 --> 00:56:42,300
just for changing environments

1039
00:56:42,300 --> 00:56:44,800
It can also actually correct for model errors

1040
00:56:44,800 --> 00:56:47,520
And so generally, this sort of approach

1041
00:56:47,520 --> 00:56:50,700
if your model is accurate in a large part of the state space

1042
00:56:50,700 --> 00:56:53,460
but in some parts of the state space, it's inaccurate

1043
00:56:53,460 --> 00:56:55,040
this sort of approach ends up actually

1044
00:56:55,040 --> 00:56:58,970
being a lot more robust to those kinds of model errors

1045
00:56:58,970 --> 00:57:00,180
Yeah, that's a good question

1046
00:57:00,180 --> 00:57:02,760
So if there are multiple correct paths

1047
00:57:02,760 --> 00:57:07,040
the question is, in that kind of multi-modality

1048
00:57:07,040 --> 00:57:11,560
will that lead to oscillation between those different paths

1049
00:57:11,560 --> 00:57:14,700
It certainly can lead to that if you're replanning all the time

1050
00:57:14,700 --> 00:57:16,760
And you might start going in one direction

1051
00:57:16,760 --> 00:57:18,390
And then maybe say like, oh, maybe I'll

1052
00:57:18,390 --> 00:57:20,360
go in a different direction

1053
00:57:20,360 --> 00:57:23,600
Sometimes, you also won't see that if the state kind of

1054
00:57:23,600 --> 00:57:27,060
indicates that you've clearly committed to one direction

1055
00:57:27,060 --> 00:57:28,100
So sometimes, you see it

1056
00:57:28,100 --> 00:57:29,320
Sometimes, you don't

1057
00:57:29,320 --> 00:57:32,680
The other way to accommodate this and also actually address

1058
00:57:32,680 --> 00:57:34,640
computational challenges is that you

1059
00:57:34,640 --> 00:57:39,400
can warm start your plan for any given time step

1060
00:57:39,400 --> 00:57:42,630
to, basically, the plan from the previous time step

1061
00:57:42,630 --> 00:57:46,150
And so if you had planned at to at plus H

1062
00:57:46,150 --> 00:57:50,830
and that the next time step you are planning--

1063
00:57:50,830 --> 00:57:55,870
based off of st plus 1, you're planning at plus 1

1064
00:57:55,870 --> 00:58:00,510
to t plus 1 plus H, then what you can do

1065
00:58:00,510 --> 00:58:05,670
is you can actually warm start this using

1066
00:58:05,670 --> 00:58:10,890
everything but the first time step of this previous plan

1067
00:58:10,890 --> 00:58:12,930
and then randomly initialize the last action

1068
00:58:12,930 --> 00:58:16,110
And you'll already have some kind of initial plan

1069
00:58:16,110 --> 00:58:17,250
that is sensible

1070
00:58:17,250 --> 00:58:20,550
And so you don't have to run as long of an optimization

1071
00:58:20,550 --> 00:58:24,630
procedure

1072
00:58:24,630 --> 00:58:28,270
And it also helps you commit to the previous action

1073
00:58:28,270 --> 00:58:31,110
in the previous plan

1074
00:58:31,110 --> 00:58:31,870
Yeah

1075
00:58:31,870 --> 00:58:33,870
Instead of randomly initializing--

1076
00:58:33,870 --> 00:58:36,350
for example, in our sampling-based thing

1077
00:58:36,350 --> 00:58:37,790
you could--

1078
00:58:37,790 --> 00:58:40,560
either here or in a sampling-based approach

1079
00:58:40,560 --> 00:58:42,760
you could initialize close to the previous plan

1080
00:58:42,760 --> 00:58:44,860
You could also add some noise or add some variance

1081
00:58:44,860 --> 00:58:48,660
to your sampling distribution so that you're not only considering

1082
00:58:48,660 --> 00:58:52,950
that particular plan

1083
00:58:52,950 --> 00:58:55,280
So replanning helps with non-deterministic environments

1084
00:58:55,280 --> 00:58:59,260
It also is really, really useful for model errors

1085
00:58:59,260 --> 00:59:01,040
It is also compute-intensive

1086
00:59:01,040 --> 00:59:05,100
So warm starting can be helpful

1087
00:59:05,100 --> 00:59:07,720
If you're actually replanning it every single time step

1088
00:59:07,720 --> 00:59:09,300
it means that every single time step

1089
00:59:09,300 --> 00:59:11,860
you're kind of reconsidering what action to take

1090
00:59:11,860 --> 00:59:15,900
And so it also can introduce additional compute challenges

1091
00:59:15,900 --> 00:59:16,900
as well

1092
00:59:16,900 --> 00:59:19,340
Sometimes, you have something in the middle where you'll

1093
00:59:19,340 --> 00:59:20,900
have some planning horizon

1094
00:59:20,900 --> 00:59:25,220
But then maybe you'll replan not every one step but every three

1095
00:59:25,220 --> 00:59:26,780
steps or every 10 steps or something

1096
00:59:26,780 --> 00:59:28,780
like that, where maybe your horizon is

1097
00:59:28,780 --> 00:59:31,190
something like 100 time steps

1098
00:59:31,190 --> 00:59:32,520
So it's an interesting question

1099
00:59:32,520 --> 00:59:34,850
So the question was like, as you're learning and getting

1100
00:59:34,850 --> 00:59:37,300
more data, presumably your model is going to get better

1101
00:59:37,300 --> 00:59:39,320
And you have more confidence in your model

1102
00:59:39,320 --> 00:59:42,740
And so maybe insofar as this is really useful for correcting

1103
00:59:42,740 --> 00:59:46,780
for model errors, you might be able to replan less frequently

1104
00:59:46,780 --> 00:59:49,260
when you have a better model

1105
00:59:49,260 --> 00:59:53,920
So that is certainly very viable

1106
00:59:53,920 --> 00:59:55,420
And usually, at some point, you will

1107
00:59:55,420 --> 00:59:57,620
have some kind of final model that you're using

1108
00:59:57,620 --> 00:59:58,840
after the learning process

1109
00:59:58,840 --> 01:00:02,300
And you could use different hyperparameters with regard

1110
01:00:02,300 --> 01:00:04,620
to replanning for that final policy

1111
01:00:04,620 --> 01:00:07,180
versus during the learning process

1112
01:00:07,180 --> 01:00:10,460
And during the learning process, it can also be valuable--

1113
01:00:10,460 --> 01:00:13,120
the model errors also help you explore to some extent

1114
01:00:13,120 --> 01:00:14,620
because you're going to be exploring

1115
01:00:14,620 --> 01:00:17,020
exactly the regions where the model is inaccurate

1116
01:00:17,020 --> 01:00:21,500
because you'll be incentivized to exploit any inaccuracies

1117
01:00:21,500 --> 01:00:23,900
So the question is, it seems like this

1118
01:00:23,900 --> 01:00:25,560
is an off-policy algorithm

1119
01:00:25,560 --> 01:00:31,140
And does it make sense how do you choose that policy

1120
01:00:31,140 --> 01:00:33,200
So yes, this is an off-policy algorithm

1121
01:00:33,200 --> 01:00:37,810
We don't need to be using data from our current policy

1122
01:00:37,810 --> 01:00:39,710
You can actually use a pretty broad policy

1123
01:00:39,710 --> 01:00:43,810
And it makes sense to use a pretty broad policy because then

1124
01:00:43,810 --> 01:00:47,210
you'll get a model that's accurate in more places

1125
01:00:47,210 --> 01:00:49,050
assuming that your model is expressive

1126
01:00:49,050 --> 01:00:54,500
and can fit that broader data distribution

1127
01:00:54,500 --> 01:00:56,490
So far, we've covered planning with models

1128
01:00:56,490 --> 01:00:57,870
So we plan a sequence of actions

1129
01:00:57,870 --> 01:01:00,850
We can do this either with gradient descent or sampling

1130
01:01:00,850 --> 01:01:04,130
We then update the model using data collected

1131
01:01:04,130 --> 01:01:06,630
through this planning process

1132
01:01:06,630 --> 01:01:10,970
And we can replan to help account for mistakes

1133
01:01:10,970 --> 01:01:15,850
This is a pretty simple way to use a learned model

1134
01:01:15,850 --> 01:01:18,250
One of the things that's, I think, really cool about this

1135
01:01:18,250 --> 01:01:21,170
is if you have a model that's quite good

1136
01:01:21,170 --> 01:01:23,330
you can actually plug in different rewards

1137
01:01:23,330 --> 01:01:26,650
or different goals at test time even and get

1138
01:01:26,650 --> 01:01:29,410
a policy that can follow--

1139
01:01:29,410 --> 01:01:34,490
for example, if you have a model that's universally good

1140
01:01:34,490 --> 01:01:37,610
and you have a robot that's trying to walk, then

1141
01:01:37,610 --> 01:01:39,570
with that same exact model, you can

1142
01:01:39,570 --> 01:01:41,150
use this process at test time and say

1143
01:01:41,150 --> 01:01:42,310
I want you to run forwards

1144
01:01:42,310 --> 01:01:43,790
I want you to walk backwards

1145
01:01:43,790 --> 01:01:45,330
I want you to crouch

1146
01:01:45,330 --> 01:01:52,610
And this kind of model is quite task-agnostic

1147
01:01:52,610 --> 01:01:54,990
The downside is that it's compute-intensive at test time

1148
01:01:54,990 --> 01:01:57,390
And so you need to account for this

1149
01:01:57,390 --> 01:02:00,230
And if you have an expensive model

1150
01:02:00,230 --> 01:02:03,050
an expensive planning process, this kind

1151
01:02:03,050 --> 01:02:06,850
of planning at test time might not be suitable

1152
01:02:06,850 --> 01:02:09,330
And the last thing is we've been talking about this

1153
01:02:09,330 --> 01:02:11,070
for short horizon problems

1154
01:02:11,070 --> 01:02:14,410
This is not applicable to long horizon problems

1155
01:02:14,410 --> 01:02:17,130
unless you have a shaped reward function

1156
01:02:17,130 --> 01:02:20,930
So if you have a shaped reward function that is telling you

1157
01:02:20,930 --> 01:02:24,250
a lot about what a good state is versus what a bad state is--

1158
01:02:24,250 --> 01:02:27,450
for example, maybe you have an infinite horizon problem

1159
01:02:27,450 --> 01:02:29,180
that you want to walk forward forever

1160
01:02:29,180 --> 01:02:31,550
You want to learn a policy that can walk forward forever

1161
01:02:31,550 --> 01:02:33,260
But you have a very dense reward function

1162
01:02:33,260 --> 01:02:35,520
that's like forward velocity

1163
01:02:35,520 --> 01:02:37,640
Then you can plan for a short horizon

1164
01:02:37,640 --> 01:02:42,040
in a way that approximates the long horizon problem

1165
01:02:42,040 --> 01:02:44,640
But if you have a long horizon problem that

1166
01:02:44,640 --> 01:02:47,080
doesn't have a very dense reward signal

1167
01:02:47,080 --> 01:02:52,040
then this is not a good approach

1168
01:02:52,040 --> 01:02:57,100
And part of the reason for this is the accuracy of the model

1169
01:02:57,100 --> 01:02:59,600
Another part of this is that it's

1170
01:02:59,600 --> 01:03:03,280
going to be expensive to be planning out a very long horizon

1171
01:03:03,280 --> 01:03:07,000
sequence of actions because you're not

1172
01:03:07,000 --> 01:03:15,590
learning a policy in this case

1173
01:03:15,590 --> 01:03:20,480
Now, the question is, can we train a policy using this model

1174
01:03:20,480 --> 01:03:24,440
And the first option here is you could actually

1175
01:03:24,440 --> 01:03:27,140
distill a planner's actions into a policy

1176
01:03:27,140 --> 01:03:30,440
So you could store these actions and then train a policy

1177
01:03:30,440 --> 01:03:35,410
to match these actions

1178
01:03:35,410 --> 01:03:38,260
This would address the compute-intensive problem

1179
01:03:38,260 --> 01:03:40,470
And this would make it a lot cheaper because then you

1180
01:03:40,470 --> 01:03:42,890
just need to run a forward pass through the neural network

1181
01:03:42,890 --> 01:03:45,510
to get your actions

1182
01:03:45,510 --> 01:03:48,290
This doesn't address the horizon problem, though

1183
01:03:48,290 --> 01:03:51,590
because we're still going to be using this procedure

1184
01:03:51,590 --> 01:03:55,150
to optimize for actions

1185
01:03:55,150 --> 01:03:57,310
And then in terms of using models

1186
01:03:57,310 --> 01:04:04,630
for longer horizon problems, there's two main solutions

1187
01:04:04,630 --> 01:04:07,870
The first solution is that right now, we're

1188
01:04:07,870 --> 01:04:14,590
optimizing for a sum of rewards from t to t plus H

1189
01:04:14,590 --> 01:04:18,390
And if we want to optimize for a longer horizon of rewards

1190
01:04:18,390 --> 01:04:20,710
so far the way that we would have

1191
01:04:20,710 --> 01:04:24,290
to do that is by optimizing over a longer horizon of actions

1192
01:04:24,290 --> 01:04:26,810
which gets higher and higher dimensional

1193
01:04:26,810 --> 01:04:28,270
But another thing that you could do

1194
01:04:28,270 --> 01:04:33,430
is that if you have a value function or an estimate

1195
01:04:33,430 --> 01:04:35,550
of a value function, then you could actually

1196
01:04:35,550 --> 01:04:39,630
use that value function to help this process, where instead

1197
01:04:39,630 --> 01:04:43,750
of trying to maximize the sum of rewards for the next H time

1198
01:04:43,750 --> 01:04:46,270
steps, you could optimize for the sum of rewards

1199
01:04:46,270 --> 01:04:51,630
for the next time steps plus your estimate of the value

1200
01:04:51,630 --> 01:04:58,790
of the state that you end up at after taking those H actions

1201
01:04:58,790 --> 01:05:02,910
And so this would end up with an algorithm

1202
01:05:02,910 --> 01:05:05,950
where you're learning both a model and a value function

1203
01:05:05,950 --> 01:05:09,030
And then you would be running this planning process

1204
01:05:09,030 --> 01:05:11,070
You'd be considering a bunch of different actions

1205
01:05:11,070 --> 01:05:13,740
over some horizon and then pass that horizon

1206
01:05:13,740 --> 01:05:15,370
You're just asking your value function

1207
01:05:15,370 --> 01:05:17,870
is that a good state or a bad state

1208
01:05:17,870 --> 01:05:23,510
And so this is a valid way to combine planning

1209
01:05:23,510 --> 01:05:31,020
with value-based RL in order to select actions

1210
01:05:31,020 --> 01:05:34,240
So the question is, why use the model

1211
01:05:34,240 --> 01:05:40,340
Why not just use a Q-function, for example

1212
01:05:40,340 --> 01:05:42,340
This is why I didn't want to spend too much time

1213
01:05:42,340 --> 01:05:46,460
on this method, is that in this case, you have to learn a value

1214
01:05:46,460 --> 01:05:47,620
function and a model

1215
01:05:47,620 --> 01:05:49,370
You're learning a lot of different things

1216
01:05:49,370 --> 01:05:51,500
And there's a lot of moving pieces

1217
01:05:51,500 --> 01:05:53,640
There is still some merit to using this

1218
01:05:53,640 --> 01:05:57,300
And for example, if you know the model

1219
01:05:57,300 --> 01:05:59,260
then you're going to get a better

1220
01:05:59,260 --> 01:06:02,920
estimate of future rewards than your value function

1221
01:06:02,920 --> 01:06:07,300
And so you can actually use this to plan out future actions

1222
01:06:07,300 --> 01:06:09,460
Like, in games, it's actually really common

1223
01:06:09,460 --> 01:06:12,980
to do these rollouts through your model

1224
01:06:12,980 --> 01:06:18,180
of the game to the extent that it's computationally feasible

1225
01:06:18,180 --> 01:06:21,760
and then use your value function just because it's

1226
01:06:21,760 --> 01:06:22,840
going to be more accurate

1227
01:06:22,840 --> 01:06:24,510
It's going to give you a better estimate

1228
01:06:24,510 --> 01:06:27,070
of how good your action is

1229
01:06:27,070 --> 01:06:28,950
But it's only if you have a really good model

1230
01:06:28,950 --> 01:06:34,210
or if it's practical and not too difficult to learn a good model

1231
01:06:34,210 --> 01:06:40,060
And then the other approach, which I'm thinking--

1232
01:06:40,060 --> 01:06:41,230
we don't have too much time

1233
01:06:41,230 --> 01:06:50,810
And so I think I might cover this second approach on Friday

1234
01:06:50,810 --> 01:06:58,050
and then go through the case study

1235
01:06:58,050 --> 01:07:06,630
So let's talk about this case study

1236
01:07:06,630 --> 01:07:08,130
So this case study is actually going

1237
01:07:08,130 --> 01:07:11,890
to be an example of using planning

1238
01:07:11,890 --> 01:07:15,090
for dexterous manipulation

1239
01:07:15,090 --> 01:07:18,890
And the reason why I want to cover this case study briefly

1240
01:07:18,890 --> 01:07:21,010
is that in my mind, it's actually still

1241
01:07:21,010 --> 01:07:22,970
one of the most impressive robotics results

1242
01:07:22,970 --> 01:07:24,970
with a five-fingered hand

1243
01:07:24,970 --> 01:07:27,610
And it also has some pretty nice comparisons

1244
01:07:27,610 --> 01:07:29,730
that are elucidating about what is

1245
01:07:29,730 --> 01:07:34,970
important for good performance in model-based RL and planning

1246
01:07:34,970 --> 01:07:37,010
So the way it works is the states

1247
01:07:37,010 --> 01:07:40,850
are the position of the hand and the position of objects

1248
01:07:40,850 --> 01:07:45,110
This assumes that you know the 3D position of an object

1249
01:07:45,110 --> 01:07:49,660
which you can estimate with a computer vision system or put

1250
01:07:49,660 --> 01:07:54,450
motion capture markers on the objects

1251
01:07:54,450 --> 01:07:57,090
whereas the hand position, you can use sensors in the hand

1252
01:07:57,090 --> 01:08:00,170
itself in order to measure the hand positions

1253
01:08:00,170 --> 01:08:02,350
The action space is to control a five-fingered hand

1254
01:08:02,350 --> 01:08:07,010
It has 24 different joints that you can control

1255
01:08:07,010 --> 01:08:12,010
And the reward is to target some kind of object trajectory, where

1256
01:08:12,010 --> 01:08:17,370
there's also some penalty for dropping the object

1257
01:08:17,370 --> 01:08:21,210
The model is going to be actually pretty simple

1258
01:08:21,210 --> 01:08:23,770
The model is going to be a neural network with two hidden

1259
01:08:23,770 --> 01:08:26,040
layers of size 500

1260
01:08:26,040 --> 01:08:29,439
But it's also going to train an ensemble of neural networks

1261
01:08:29,439 --> 01:08:32,479
And this is an example that I wanted

1262
01:08:32,479 --> 01:08:34,680
to look at because this kind of ensembling approach

1263
01:08:34,680 --> 01:08:36,399
can also help with model errors

1264
01:08:36,399 --> 01:08:38,479
And the way this works is instead of just training

1265
01:08:38,479 --> 01:08:40,760
one neural network, you'll train three neural networks

1266
01:08:40,760 --> 01:08:42,600
with different random initializations

1267
01:08:42,600 --> 01:08:46,040
and different kind of mini batch sampling, just

1268
01:08:46,040 --> 01:08:47,680
with different random seeds

1269
01:08:47,680 --> 01:08:51,040
And then if one model is very optimistic about what

1270
01:08:51,040 --> 01:08:53,200
will happen, that can be counterbalanced

1271
01:08:53,200 --> 01:08:55,899
by errors in other models

1272
01:08:55,899 --> 01:09:02,840
And it essentially gives you a way to deal with the fact

1273
01:09:02,840 --> 01:09:08,160
that models will have different errors in different ways

1274
01:09:08,160 --> 01:09:12,399
So you're not going to exploit the errors in any one model

1275
01:09:12,399 --> 01:09:14,560
And then the planner is a modified version

1276
01:09:14,560 --> 01:09:15,819
of the cross-entropy method

1277
01:09:15,819 --> 01:09:18,960
So it's going to be using iterative resampling

1278
01:09:18,960 --> 01:09:20,439
of the actions

1279
01:09:20,439 --> 01:09:25,720
And the modifications are that it's

1280
01:09:25,720 --> 01:09:29,540
going to use this softer reward weighted mean

1281
01:09:29,540 --> 01:09:31,000
And it does some sort of temporal

1282
01:09:31,000 --> 01:09:33,760
smoothing on the actions to encourage and search

1283
01:09:33,760 --> 01:09:41,060
over actions that are taking smoother trajectories

1284
01:09:41,060 --> 01:09:42,439
But you can basically think of it

1285
01:09:42,439 --> 01:09:44,040
as cross-entropy method or this kind

1286
01:09:44,040 --> 01:09:46,560
of iterative sampling-based optimization

1287
01:09:46,560 --> 01:09:49,319
And then they alternate between, in step 1

1288
01:09:49,319 --> 01:09:57,920
collecting 30 trajectories and updating the model

1289
01:09:57,920 --> 01:09:59,840
So then in the simulated experiments

1290
01:09:59,840 --> 01:10:02,740
we are going to see a comparison to some model free methods

1291
01:10:02,740 --> 01:10:05,280
methods that are not using a dynamics model

1292
01:10:05,280 --> 01:10:07,360
We'll see [? soft ?] actor critic and an algorithm

1293
01:10:07,360 --> 01:10:09,800
called natural policy gradient, which is a policy gradient

1294
01:10:09,800 --> 01:10:10,760
method

1295
01:10:10,760 --> 01:10:12,960
And then we'll also see some model-based methods

1296
01:10:12,960 --> 01:10:16,040
So the proposed method is called PDDM

1297
01:10:16,040 --> 01:10:18,200
We'll also see an algorithm called

1298
01:10:18,200 --> 01:10:23,030
MBPO, a different kind of planner called PETS

1299
01:10:23,030 --> 01:10:25,630
and then also a method that doesn't use ensembles

1300
01:10:25,630 --> 01:10:29,110
and doesn't use iterative sampling

1301
01:10:29,110 --> 01:10:31,510
And here are some videos of what you're

1302
01:10:31,510 --> 01:10:34,130
able to do with this method

1303
01:10:34,130 --> 01:10:40,710
So we're running planning in the loop of actually execution

1304
01:10:40,710 --> 01:10:44,110
And we see that it's able to learn

1305
01:10:44,110 --> 01:10:47,030
how to rotate the bounding balls in the hand

1306
01:10:47,030 --> 01:10:52,110
and also write digits on this piece of paper

1307
01:10:52,110 --> 01:10:56,430
And we see that if we plot the kind of reward where higher

1308
01:10:56,430 --> 01:10:59,270
is better over the number of data points

1309
01:10:59,270 --> 01:11:01,670
that are collected in order to fit the model

1310
01:11:01,670 --> 01:11:05,030
or in the policy in the case of model free methods

1311
01:11:05,030 --> 01:11:08,230
we see that, first, for this really complex task, only

1312
01:11:08,230 --> 01:11:10,590
the proposed method is able to solve it because it's

1313
01:11:10,590 --> 01:11:12,240
really challenging

1314
01:11:12,240 --> 01:11:14,950
And we also see that some of the methods that, for example, don't

1315
01:11:14,950 --> 01:11:17,790
do iterative sampling, that, for example, just do

1316
01:11:17,790 --> 01:11:19,990
random sampling, shown in yellow

1317
01:11:19,990 --> 01:11:22,340
get very poor performance because you're not

1318
01:11:22,340 --> 01:11:26,180
able to get to a good part of the distribution

1319
01:11:26,180 --> 01:11:28,900
and hone in on good actions

1320
01:11:28,900 --> 01:11:32,340
And in the case of handwriting, which is a little bit easier

1321
01:11:32,340 --> 01:11:35,180
we see that some methods are able to get good performance

1322
01:11:35,180 --> 01:11:38,720
